nformation that can help management make decisions.\n\n\n---\n\n3. What is Data Analysis?\n\nData Analysis is the process of:\n\n> Collecting → Cleaning → Exploring → Visualizing → Drawing Conclusions → Making Decisions\n\n\n\nA data analyst answers questions like:\n\nWhy are sales decreasing?\n\nWhich products sell the most?\n\nWhich customers generate the highest profit?\n\nWhich machine produces the most defects?\n\nWhich department performs best?\n\n\n\n---\n\n4. Data Analysis Workflow\n\nRaw Data\n    ↓\nData Cleaning\n    ↓\nExploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n    ↓\nVisualization\n    ↓\nStatistical Analysis\n    ↓\nBusiness Insights\n    ↓\nDecision Making\n\nThis workflow will be followed throughout the course.\n\n\n---\n\n5. Libraries We'll Use\n\nIn this course, we'll primarily use:\n\nLibrary\tPurpose\n\nNumPy\tNumerical computations\nPandas\tData manipulation\nMatplotlib\tBasic visualization\nSeaborn\tStatistical visualization\nScikit-learn\tBuilt-in datasets and machine learning\n\n\n\n---\n\n6. Your First Python Code\n\nOpen a new Jupyter Notebook and type:\n\nprint(\"Welcome to Data Analysis\")\n\nOutput\n\nWelcome to Data Analysis\n\nExplanation\n\nprint() displays output on the screen.\n\n\n---\n\n7. Import Libraries\n\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\nExplanation\n\npandas → Data handling.\n\nnumpy → Numerical operations.\n\nmatplotlib → Basic charts.\n\nseaborn → Built-in datasets and attractive visualizations.\n\n\n\n---\n\n8. Load Your First Dataset\n\nSeaborn provides several built-in datasets. We'll start with the Tips dataset, which contains information about restaurant bills and tips.\n\ntips = sns.load_dataset(\"tips\")\n\nWhat happened?\n\nsns is the alias for the Seaborn library.\n\nload_dataset() loads a sample dataset.\n\n\"tips\" specifies which dataset to load.\n\nThe dataset is stored in the variable tips.\n\n\nNothing is displayed because Jupyter only shows output when you ask it to.\n\n\n---\n\n9. View the Dataset\n\ntips\n\nOutput (first few rows)\n\ntotal_bill\ttip\tsex\tsmoker\tday\ttime\tsize\n\n16.99\t1.01\tFemale\tNo\tSun\tDinner\t2\n10.34\t1.66\tMale\tNo\tSun\tDinner\t3\n21.01\t3.50\tMale\tNo\tSun\tDinner\t3\n\n\nObservation: Each row represents a restaurant bill.\n\n\n---\n\n10. Display the First Five Rows\n\ntips.head()\n\nWhy use .head()?\n\nDatasets can have thousands or millions of rows. Viewing everything is impractical. .head() quickly previews the first five rows.\n\n\ntips.tail()\ntips.shape\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 13. Display Column Names","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"raw","source":"tips.columns","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 14. Check Data Types","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"raw","source":"tips.dtypes","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 16. Business Thinking","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-21T05:41:15.763584Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-21T05:41:15.763893Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-21T05:41:15.768879Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-21T05:41:15.763869Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-21T05:41:15.767606Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":6},{"cell_type":"raw","source":"Imagine you're the owner of the restaurant. You might ask:\n-What is the average bill?\n-What is the average tip?\n–Do smokers tip more?\n–Which day has the highest revenue?\n–Does table size affect the tip amount?\n–These questions are exactly what data analysts investigate.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Practice Exercises","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-21T05:40:37.996202Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-21T05:40:37.996459Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-21T05:40:38.000583Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-21T05:40:37.996435Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-21T05:40:37.999768Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":5},{"cell_type":"raw","source":"1. Load the tips dataset.\n2. Display the first 10 rows using tips.head(10).\n3. Display the last 8 rows using tips.tail(8).\n4. Find the dataset shape.\n5. Display the column names.\n6. Check the data types.\n7. Generate the summary statistics with tips.describe().","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Interview Questions","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-21T05:39:06.751297Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-21T05:39:06.751603Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-21T05:39:06.756322Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-21T05:39:06.751579Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-21T05:39:06.755503Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":4},{"cell_type":"raw","source":"1. What is data analysis?\n2. What is the difference between data and information?\n3. Why do we use Pandas?\n4. What does .head() do?\n5. What information does .shape return?\n6. Why are data types important?\n7. What is the purpose of .describe()?","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}